= Transclusion and composition =

BITwiki is designed for modular, composable knowledge without fragmenting every article into a pseudo-filesystem.

== Canonical page first ==
A topic should normally have one coherent canonical page. Important semantic portions of that page can be selectively addressed or transcluded when reuse is useful.

== Book Matter as addressable anatomy ==
Book Matter gives names to recurring semantic sections such as:
* Introduction
* Definition
* Overview
* Background
* History
* Theory
* Mechanism
* Methods
* Evidence
* Results
* Examples
* Applications
* Implementation
* Limitations
* Challenges
* Criticism
* Related concepts
* See also
* Sources
* References
* Bibliography
* Further reading

A page does not need every section. Section names should reflect what the content actually contains.

== Progressive disclosure ==
Modularity should support multiple levels of reading: a concise overview, intermediate explanation, and deeper technical material where appropriate. Humans and agents should be able to retrieve only the content needed for a task without losing access to the canonical whole.

== What not to do ==
* Do not automatically create one subpage per section.
* Do not create generic Main pages named Overview, Mechanism, Applications, and similar fragments.
* Do not use categories to represent Book Matter.
* Do not make templates named after Book Matter merely because the section exists.

== Implementation independence ==
Book Matter is a content model, not a dependency on one extension. MediaWiki transclusion, templates, section-level mechanisms, or later extensions may implement selective composition as appropriate.

